# Iterator[T] is a built-in, single-pass iterator type; no import is required.
# A function containing yield must declare -> Iterator[T]. Calling it creates
# a suspended generator. Each iteration resumes it until the next yield.
#
# def count(stop: i32) -> Iterator[i32]:
#     for i in range(stop):
#         yield i
#
# for n in count(3):
#     print(n)
#
# Classes are iterable by defining __iter__(self) -> Iterator[T]. The method
# may yield values itself or return an iterator created by another function.
# Iterator values also work in list comprehensions.
#
# break preserves the iterator position; a later loop resumes it. Exhausted
# iterators stay exhausted. Iterator assignments share the same position.
# Exhaustion releases the coroutine frame. Call it.close() when abandoning
# a partially consumed iterator, including generators that never started.
# close() is idempotent and executes cleanup active at the suspended yield.
# As with other runtime containers, the iterator cell uses the selected GC.
#
# yield requires a value assignable to T. A bare return ends the generator;
# return with a value is invalid. Async generators, yield expressions, and
# yield from are not supported. Deferred and finally cleanup blocks cannot yield.
